COMDAT: a definition that may turn up in more than one object - an inline function, a template
instantiation, a vtable - goes in a section of its own, and the linker keeps one copy and drops the
rest (SELECT_ANY), or keeps a section with the one it belongs to (SELECT_ASSOCIATIVE: a function's
unwind data). ml64 has no syntax for either; this assembler takes

    .text$mn SEGMENT ALIGN(16) 'CODE' COMDAT(sym)          folded on sym, defined inside
    .pdata SEGMENT READONLY ALIGN(4) 'DATA' ASSOCIATIVE(sym)   kept with the section sym's COMDAT opened

c1.asm and c2.asm both define twice, helper and table; linked together by tests/windows/link.cmd
they run and print c.expected. In the object the section carries IMAGE_SCN_LNK_COMDAT, its section
symbol's auxiliary record the selection (2 or 5, with the associated section's number), and the key
symbol follows the section symbol, which is where the linker looks for it - dumpbin shows
"selection 2 (pick any)" and "(pick associative Section 0x6)". This is what lets cxx1 assemble its
x86_64-windows C++ output here instead of through clang: its MASM spelling writes these clauses.
